 "name": "portal-sweep",
 "notes": "Batch mode: ONE prepared field, ONE entry portal, repeated trips with NOTHING re-aimed. Field: bedrock x 0..11, z -4..4 at y -64 in the void overworld (rows -4 and 4 are the border rows a site needs on each side). Each trip an item goes through the same Nether portal; between trips only the generated portal's portal blocks are removed (a player mines one frame block), the rest of its frame is LEFT. The leftover frame blocks its own row and the rows beside it, so the game's closest-site rule moves each trip to a fresh line by itself. Pass 1 (exit on row 0): 9 trips convert rows 0, 2, -2 across the whole width. Then the leftover frames are mined and the exit moved one row (item 1/8 block further): pass 2, 12 trips, converts rows 1, -1, 3, -3. Every bedrock of rows -3..3 is obsidian at the end. Asserted against reference slabs: after pass 1 rows 0, 2, -2 are entirely obsidian and rows 1, -1, 3, -3 entirely bedrock; after pass 2 rows -3..3 are entirely obsidian and the border rows entirely bedrock.",
